---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { agents } from '../data/agents';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/102896351/Agents';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="AI Agents Hub is an independent, open-source directory of AI agents. All listing data is public and open to corrections on GitHub."
>
  <section class="hero" style="padding-top: 56px;">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>About <span class="grad">AI Agents Hub</span></h1>
      <p>An independent, open-source directory of AI agents — built and maintained by a single developer.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 720px;">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What this is</h2>
        <p>
          AI Agents Hub is a side project by an independent developer, not a publication,
          a company, or a newsroom. It is a static site: the {agents.length} agent profiles
          and the comparison guides are generated from plain data files in a
          <a href={REPO} target="_blank" rel="noopener">public GitHub repository</a>.
          There is no editorial team and no review board.
        </p>
        <p>
          The point of the site is comparability. Every agent gets the same fields —
          what it does, license, hosting, models, pricing, strengths, weaknesses, and a
          direct "pick this if / skip this if" verdict — so you can scan several tools
          side by side instead of reading ten different marketing pages.
        </p>

        <h2>Why this exists</h2>
        <p>
          The AI agent landscape moves fast. Existing directories tend to be either
          overwhelming (thousands of entries, no signal) or promotional (every tool
          is "the best"). This site aims for a middle ground: a tight list with
          structured, honest notes on each.
        </p>

        <h2>How agents are selected</h2>
        <p>A listing is added when it clears four basic checks:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Real traction</strong> — active development and a genuine user base.</li>
          <li><strong>Clear use case</strong> — you should know what it's for in one sentence.</li>
          <li><strong>Accessible</strong> — you can actually try it, not just watch a demo.</li>
          <li><strong>Distinct</strong> — it does something the others in its category don't.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="how-profiles-are-written">How the profiles are written</h2>
        <p>
          Profiles are <strong>compiled from public sources</strong> — the project's
          official docs, README, repository, changelog, pricing page, and published
          benchmark reports — and normalized into a consistent template. The verdict,
          pros/cons, and "pick if / skip if" notes are the maintainer's own judgement
          based on those sources.
        </p>
        <p>To be clear about what this site does <em>not</em> claim:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Entries are <strong>not hands-on lab tests</strong>. The sample inputs and
            outputs shown on a profile are <strong>illustrative examples</strong>, not
            measured runs.
          </li>
          <li>
            Benchmark figures are quoted from public sources and can be outdated or
            measured under different conditions. Check the original source before
            relying on a number.
          </li>
          <li>
            Pricing, model support, and license terms change frequently. A profile is
            a starting point for research, not an authoritative or real-time record.
          </li>
          <li>
            There is no fixed re-review schedule and no guaranteed correction
            turnaround. This is a one-person project maintained in spare time.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What the badges mean</h2>
        <p>
          <span class="badge oss">Open Source</span> — code is public and self-hostable. &nbsp;
          <span class="badge commercial">Commercial</span> — paid product, often closed-source. &nbsp;
          <span class="badge freemium">Freemium</span> — free tier with paid upgrades.
        </p>

        <h2>Independence</h2>
        <p>
          Listings are not paid placements and there are no sponsored rankings.
          A "Featured" label is an unpaid, independent choice based on public
          information. If a listing is ever sponsored, it will be disclosed clearly
          on the listing itself.
        </p>

        <h2 id="contributing">Open data &amp; contributing</h2>
        <!-- Legacy anchor: older pages linked to #editorial-team. Keep the URL working. -->
        <span id="editorial-team"></span>
        <p>
          The whole directory is one data set in the open. That is deliberate: anyone
          can audit a claim, fix a stale number, or add a missing agent without
          waiting for a site owner to reply.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Agent profiles</strong> — <code>src/data/agents.ts</code> and the <code>agents-extra-*.ts</code> files.</li>
          <li><strong>Comparison guides</strong> — <code>src/data/blog.ts</code> and the related data files.</li>
          <li><strong>Profile images</strong> — <code>public/agents/</code>, with the original source linked in each caption.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Open an <a href={`${REPO}/issues`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">issue</a> or send a
          <a href={`${REPO}/pulls`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">pull request</a>. Useful
          contributions include a corrected price or benchmark with a source link, a
          missing agent, a fixed dead link, or a corrected sample. Changes are reviewed
          and merged in public; the commit history is the change log.
        </p>

        <h2>Corrections</h2>
        <p>
          Found a wrong price, a stale benchmark, or a misattributed quote? The fastest
          fix is a pull request or an issue with a link to the authoritative source.
          You can also <a href="/contact/">email the maintainer</a>. There is no promised
          response time, but public issues stay visible until handled.
        </p>

        <div class="verdict-box" style="margin-top: 32px;">
          <div class="label">Note</div>
          <p style="margin: 0; color: var(--text);">
            AI Agents Hub is an independent project. All product names, logos, and brands
            are property of their respective owners. Screenshots are used for
            identification and review purposes, with their source linked in the caption.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
